---
import TsumlyLayout from '../layouts/TsumlyLayout.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, '/');
const siteHome = 'https://nadeemdevapps.github.io/my-web/';

// Short Person JSON-LD for SEO/AEO — no location stated.
const personLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Person',
  name: 'Nadeem Mazhar',
  jobTitle: 'Solo Developer',
  description:
    'Solo developer. Builds websites, Windows apps, Android apps, and backends — local-first or cloud, whichever the project needs — with AI doing the heavy lifting under his direction.',
  url: `https://nadeemdevapps.github.io${base}about/`,
  sameAs: ['https://github.com/nadeemdevapps'],
};
---

<TsumlyLayout
  title="About Tsumly — A Ledger That Finally Adds Up"
  description="Why Tsumly exists: money lent and borrowed between people deserves a record you can trust — local-first, no account, free. The story, the name, and what makes it different."
  jsonLd={[personLd]}
>
  <!-- ============ STORY ============ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <div class="reveal">
        <span class="kicker">About</span>
        <h1 class="page-h1">A ledger that finally adds up.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Tsumly started as my own problem — a ledger that never added up.
          I was lending and borrowing money the way most people do: a note
          here, a memory there, a chat message somewhere else. The amounts
          were small and the people were people I trusted — and still, the
          record never quite added up. Who had paid what? What was left?
          The answer was always a guess.
        </p>
        <p class="lede">
          Money between people deserves a record you can trust. So I built
          one: a ledger for the money you lend and borrow, where every
          amount is recorded, every repayment lowers what is left, and the
          balance is always honest. It lives on your phone, it works
          offline, it needs no account — and it is free.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ WHY THE NAME ============ -->
  <section class="section alt">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <div class="reveal">
        <span class="kicker">Why the name</span>
        <h2>Why "Tsumly"?</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          The name is inspired by the Japanese verb <strong>tsumu</strong>
          (積む) — to stack, to accumulate, to build up over time.
        </p>
        <p class="lede">
          Every amount stacks onto the last — a loan, a repayment, a rupee
          at a time — until the ledger is complete. Small amounts, recorded
          honestly, accumulate into the full picture: what you are owed,
          and what you owe. Over weeks and months, the ledger builds up
          into a record you can trust — and a history you can share.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ WHAT MAKES IT DIFFERENT ============ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <div class="reveal">
        <span class="kicker">What makes it different</span>
        <h2>Three things most ledger apps get wrong — or leave out.</h2>
        <ul class="feature-list">
          <li><strong>Smart repayment split</strong> — when someone pays you back, the money rarely matches a single loan. Tsumly splits one repayment across several loans in a single action — and never merges them, so each loan keeps its own history. <a href={`${base}features/#smart-repayment-split`}>How the split works →</a></li>
          <li><strong>Pre-app loans</strong> — you were lending and borrowing long before Tsumly. Pre-app loans record those older loans without moving your current balance, so your history is complete and your balance stays honest. <a href={`${base}features/#pre-app-loans`}>How pre-app loans work →</a></li>
          <li><strong>Person profiles</strong> — loans live on person profiles, so you always see who owes what: per-person totals, a guarded archive, and a full ledger per person. <a href={`${base}features/#person-profiles`}>How profiles work →</a></li>
        </ul>
        <p class="lede">
          Around those three, Tsumly tracks the everyday money too —
          income, expenses and transfers in the same ledger — with monthly
          reports, statements you can share, and reminders that arrive on
          their own. <a href={`${base}features/`}>Every feature, in detail →</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ SHAPED BY COMPARISON ============ -->
  <section class="section alt">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <div class="reveal">
        <span class="kicker">Shaped by comparison</span>
        <h2>Compared against the apps out there — and against daily use.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Tsumly was shaped by comparing it with other ledger apps. Testing
          the app on my own phone against <strong>Pendara</strong>, for
          example, led to three changes: the people list got cards like
          the accounts list, the person detail got a single summary card
          instead of three confusing tiles, and onboarding gained a
          restore step, so a fresh install can bring back a backup before
          anything else.
        </p>
        <p class="lede">
          That is how Tsumly keeps being shaped — by using it every day,
          and by what is missing. If a repayment cannot be split honestly,
          or an old loan has nowhere honest to live, that is the next
          thing to fix.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ DEVELOPER (SHORT) ============ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <div class="reveal">
        <span class="kicker">Built by Nadeem</span>
        <h2>A solo developer, building for himself first.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Tsumly is built by <strong>Nadeem Mazhar</strong>, a solo
          developer, as <strong>Nadeem Dev Apps</strong>. I plan, design,
          and direct the build, with AI tools doing the heavy lifting —
          and every app starts as my own problem. Tsumly began as a ledger
          that never added up.
        </p>
        <p class="lede">
          Questions, feedback, or just want to say hi? Reach me at
          <a href="mailto:nadeem.devapps@gmail.com">nadeem.devapps@gmail.com</a>,
          or find my work on GitHub at
          <a href="https://github.com/nadeemdevapps">github.com/nadeemdevapps</a>
          — my only social link.
        </p>
        <p class="lede">
          <a class="about-link" href={siteHome}>More from Nadeem Dev Apps →</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <script>
    // Same restrained reveals as the home page.
    var els = document.querySelectorAll('.reveal');
    if ('IntersectionObserver' in window) {
      var io = new IntersectionObserver(function (entries) {
        entries.forEach(function (e) {
          if (e.isIntersecting) {
            e.target.classList.add('in');
            io.unobserve(e.target);
          }
        });
      }, { threshold: 0.12 });
      els.forEach(function (el) {
        io.observe(el);
      });
    } else {
      els.forEach(function (el) {
        el.classList.add('in');
      });
    }
  </script>
</TsumlyLayout>
